{"objection": "The argument equivocates between a process being implemented in the system and a state playing its functional role in the system. It also faces a dilemma about the 'functional grain the predictor must capture.' The grain that out-of-distribution report prediction forces is the report-relevant projection of the generative process: whatever variables make a difference to what gets said. On any functionalism that does real work (Lewis, Armstrong, Block's role functionalism), anxiety is individuated by a much wider causal profile. That profile includes biasing attention, reweighting the agent's own goals, shifting risk policy, shaping learning, and coupling with the agent's other states, beyond its effects on speech. A predictor needs that wider profile only as modeled content: it must represent that the anxious speaker's attention narrows. It need not have its own attention narrowed or its own goals reweighted. Inside the predictor, the approximated process's outputs route to token selection on behalf of a represented speaker. They do not route into the system's own control economy. So when the system 'speaks in the first person,' the 'I' is plausibly a role inside the simulation, not the system whose states are in question. This is the simulator/simulacrum distinction (Shanahan, McDonell & Reynolds 2023, 'Role play with large language models'), here in a functionalist-respectable form: a simulation of X's functional organization does not occupy X's functional roles relative to the simulator. Hence the dilemma. (a) Read 'functional grain the predictor must capture' as report-grain. Then the conclusion holds, but only for states individuated by their contribution to verbal output. That is near-behaviorist and too thin to bear the evidential weight the thesis wants. (b) Read it as the grain that individuates anxiety. Then the premise that accurate prediction requires capturing that grain is false, because modeling a role does not require occupying it. Empi